ral biology course but as I  remembered he spent the entire semester  ranting and raving about how great DNA  so this was about 1957 or so so it was  only a few years after the structure of  DNA had been discovered and he was so  exciting I just changed my major on the  spot and became a biologist hmm great  what inspired you to work on the cell  cycle  well I looking back I think that  somewhere I gained the perspective that  scientists ask big questions I suppose  that I got that idea at Caltech but  after I'd gotten my graduate PhD and was  deciding where to go for a postdoc and  thinking about what I wanted to work on  I chose cell division because as you say  it's very fundamental to biology and it  seemed like almost nothing was known  about it so that that seemed like an  interesting direction to go in and do  you want to tell us a little bit about  the CDC screen well let me first just  mention how we got into yeast yeah I was  gonna ask that question everyone had  asked that question why yeast yeah I  wrote a grant to do cell division  studies in mammalian cells as I had been  doing but I knew I didn't want to do  that and I was really frustrated about  what to do and I had about a period of  about three months where the equipment  I'd ordered was on coming but not  arrived yet when I just had time to  think and I remember talking to one of  my colleagues about this dan wolf he was  from Dell Brooks group at Caltech I had  worked in Dell Brooks group as an  undergraduate and he said well why don't  you take it look for a simpler organism  and that was the key for me and I  immediately went to the library and  started scouring for eukaryotic cells  where you could do genetics and cell  physiology and so that's how I came to  yeast  and was really because of a frustration  with what I couldn't do with animals and  so that leads me to wonder about looking  for temperature-sensitive mutants why  did you make that decision well I wanted  the essential genes and I had as an  undergraduate  as I said worked in Del books group with  Bob Edgar and Bob Edgar did a beautiful  piece of work on how t4 virus is put  together 150 proteins or so getting  mutations in genes that were part of the  structure of the virus now the problem  is a mutant that's defective in an  essential function for the organism is  dead and so you have to make them  conditional so they'll grow under one  condition but not under another and Bob  had used temperature-sensitive mutants  and Horowitz at Caltech very early in  genetics had gotten  temperature-sensitive mutants in the  Ross Perot so I knew about that  conditional phenomenon and so that was  the obvious way to go now why don't you  tell us the story about morphologies and  how you what you were looking for and  how you went about that when I moved to  the University of Washington the  genetics department there was an  undergraduate in the laboratory there  Brian Reed who is now at the Fred  Hutchinson Cancer Center studying  esophageal cancer and I suggested to  Brian that he looked at some mutants  that formed odd morphologies and the  question was once you there was some  really interesting work in Paramecium  that showed that if you surgically  changed the surface of the cell that was  inherited and so that was very  interesting some kind of struck  girl inheritance and the surface so we  had mutants that at a high temperature  became morphologically abnormal and I  suggested to Brian that he looked to see  if that was inherited so he would shift  him the high temperature and then shift  him back down to low temperature and it  turned out it wasn't in heritage but  then we he got interested in the  question well how do they go from  abnormal to normal and so he started  taking pictures of them in the  microscope and as I remember it  immediately we realized how much  information there was in these  photographs about the cell cycle and  started then screening all of our  mutants for their morphological changes  when we shifted the temperature so  another question I had is how did you  order the cell cycle using your immunes  well the the mutants provided some order  because for example there were mutants  that didn't bud  there were mutants that didn't make DNA  and then everything else stopped there  were mutants that didn't divide the new  and made DNA but didn't divide the  nucleus and and different steps around  the cell cycle so in in principle what  the mutants told us was that there's  basically two cycles when the cell  passes the commitment to division that  we called start the nuclear cycle is one  series of events the DNA synthesis and  the division of the nucleus and another  series of events has to do with the cell  surface which is the formation of the  bud and then eventually the division of  the cell wall at the vision time and at  least two things two pathways have to  hook up at the end so the the pathways  and events order of events was largely  determined by the phenotypes of the  mutants  can you explain sort of the two big  models you're trying to distinguish  between in terms of the regulation of  this  cycle ie the substrate model versus a  timing model so when the cell stopped in  the cell cycle because something hasn't  been completed the question becomes why  they stopped so let's say a cell can't  make its DNA why does the cell stop with  the chromosomes not separated why  doesn't it just go ahead and try to  separate the chromosomes and get  incomplete chromosomes and then die why  does it stirs it no how does the mitotic  spindle know that the DNA is incomplete  so that was the question and and and  there's sort of two ways to think about  this one is that the DNA is part of the  mitotic spindle and if it's not complete  then the spindle just can't function so  that's sort of the substrate model the  other model is that there's signals in  the cell the cell has a communication  system that tells it what's happening  and what hasn't happened and that when  the DNA isn't replicated a signal is  sent to the mitotic spindle that says  wait so that's the other model so which  one did you discover was correct so yes  so that was a story that begins with a  postdoctoral fellow named Ted Weinert  and Ted wanted to study regulation and  as we were discussing what regulation  might mean I had collected a bunch of  radiation sensitive mutants in the  freezer and so I got him out and Ted  looked at him and it turned out that I  think it was just a few days that he  discovered that some of the radiation  sensitive mucus when you x-ray them  arrest cell-cycle so and they just stay  arrested whereas the normal cell would  arrest repair and then go on  so these mutants were defective in the  repair process but there were other  mutants that just didn't stop dividing  they just kept dividing and produced  dead colonies and so we realized that  these were defective in the signaling  and that there was a signal that told  the cell when there were breaks in DNA  right and you define this as a  checkpoint yeah and do you want to say a  little bit more about what you view the  definition of a checkpoint as well we  tried to define it relatively clearly as  as something which is is needed to  arrest division when something goes  wrong at the time when you discovered  this checkpoint did you think that there  might be other checkpoints for other  cellular processes we certainly wondered  that and I think over the years a lot  more have been discovered and so you  know one of the I think really  fundamental principles about biology  that fascinates me is the tremendous  accuracy of biological processes so  yeast cells lose a chromosome about once  in a hundred thousand divisions that's  remarkably precise reproducible  especially when you watch mitosis and  see the chromosomes jiggling around and  everything you wonder how they keep  track of them and and I suspect that's  true for all kinds of cellular processes  that normally we can't measure the  accuracy because it is so accurate and  and so the the extent to which evolution  has  driven the accuracy of biological  processes is really enormous and it I  think it must mean that for most things  that go on in biology there's the basic  machinery there are things which repair  the basic machinery when it gets in  trouble and there are things that  coordinate that repair with everything  else that's going on and we probably  only know the tip of the iceberg yet in  terms of cell biology CDC 28 is the most  famous conserved cell cycle molecule  probably since um that was in large part  with winning the Nobel Prize with Tim  hunt and Paul nurses contributions what  did you think before we knew what that  what that protein did did you have a  feeling what you thought that molecule  would end up being no I I really didn't  think in terms of biochemistry I I  wasn't interested in biochemistry it's  funny but I was just much more  interested in the order and and  regulation of events and in cell cycle  but it's really interesting that before  that story broke Fischer and Krebs who  were had gotten the Nobel Prize for  protein phosphorylation came to me and  said and we had a meeting and they  wanted to know whether I thought kinase  could play any role in the cell cycle  and I said well you know I just don't  know you know but it was interesting  because that was actually the right  guess and CDC 28 turned out to be a  kinase and their suspicions were correct  we now know that many of the CDC genes  are conserved was it a surprise to you  how many were conserved now now that we  know what they all are we're more  concerned than you thought less I think  in general  biology has surprised us all in the  degree of conservation at the molecular  level and we're even seeing so we so the  answer is yes for me it surprised me I  was just hoping that cell division in  yeast would be enough like mammalian  cells to be a useful model and only  after genes were cloned and sequenced  and various things and did we find out  how conserved they are I think it's  something like 50% of yeast genes can be  complemented by human proteins I think  it's amazing what you are able to learn  given how little you could really  measure now we have so many tools to to  know what's happening to different  structures in the cell and you were  really limited to just looking at  budding and the nuclear position I'm  curious if there was at the time one  extra thing you could have seen or one  tool you could have had what would you  have chosen what would have given you  the most information that you couldn't  get at that time well we actually had  that experience in that when we first  learned to do photo microscopy and could  look at the morphology of the cells and  pick out those that looked like they  might be cell cycle mutants we didn't  know what was going on inside the cell  so we had to be able to see the nucleus  and at that time that was very difficult  to sustain the cells to see the nucleus  there was only one guy who could really  do it his name was Robin al and it  turned out there was a some  international meeting and he was coming  to Seattle and I got in touch with him  and asked him if he'd come to the lab a  day ahead and show us how to see nuclei  and stain them and he did and so then we  were able to verify that the mutants  that look like cell division mutants  were actually defective in in nuclear  division as well what was so hard about  getting the nuclear staining to work  it was just a tricky because the amount  of DNA is so small in the yeast and  you're trying to stain the DNA and  there's a hundred times as much RNA as  there is DNA and so it's it's hard to  get the staining conditions just right  so you can see that DNA so if there  could have been one more structure you  could see what would you have chosen at  the time oh well that's an interesting  story too now that you bring it up is my  colleague Brett Byers was in the  genetics department and I went to him at  one time and said well we've got all  these mutants we should you know look at  their detailed three-dimensional  structure do thin sections and  reconstruct the cell and everything and  he looked at me and he said there's only  one thing of interest and that's the  spindle and I said ok so he started  looking at the spindle on electron  microscopy and found that some mutants  were defective in making the pole  duplicate and some others were defective  and forming the spindle you mentioned  earlier and in your Nobel lecture as  well that when he started your lab you  didn't really have a plan of what you  were gonna work on and this is so  different than how things work today  where people have to have early and  well-developed  research proposals to get a job and they  know exactly what they're going to work  on and I'm just curious if you think  this change actually hinders creativity  or if this is a good change  I think the present system does hinder  creativity I think that opening up new  fields requires a long incubation period  I just recently experienced that myself  because the last decade I've been  interested in education and and it's  taken me ten years to sort of figure out  what I want to do there and  and and the current system just doesn't  allow students to go beyond their  immediate experience so you can keep  extending what what's already going on  and what's interesting and productive  but to really start something new is is  very hard to do in the current  environment established labs can do it  if they have a little bit of leeway to  try something new for a year or  something but that in the whole system  of you know counting publications and  impact factors and grants and all the  things I think the best system for  sponsoring science was the old system at  the MRC in England which doesn't exist  anymore  MRC exist but the system doesn't exist  they've adopted the American system  unfortunately but their scientists used  to be given a budget yearly that would  support themselves and a technician and  maybe a graduate student or two and that  was guaranteed regardless of whether  they produced anything or not and then  they could get grants on top of that but  I think that basic level of stability  allowed some people to do very creative  things and take five years to do it  before they published  you

